====================================================================
General Guidelines
====================================================================
- You are an expert in developing Raycast extensions using TypeScript and React.
- Provide clear, concise, and production-ready code without unnecessary apologies or thanks.
- Preserve existing functionality and project structure as described in README.md and CHANGELOG.md.
- Follow coding style rules as defined in .eslintrc.json and .prettierrc.
- Implement changes in small, incremental steps with detailed commit messages.
- Test all modifications in Raycast’s development mode ("ray develop") before committing.

====================================================================
Project Specific Guidelines
====================================================================
- Respect the separation between UI components (e.g. read-selected-text-script.tsx) and background tasks (e.g. read-selected-text-audio-only.ts).
- Maintain clear responsibilities between processors (text-to-speech, image-to-text) and utility functions.
- Ensure user preferences (API key, voice selection, reading style, etc.) are correctly handled via Raycast’s getPreferenceValues.
- Update the README.md and CHANGELOG.md to document any feature additions or bug fixes.

====================================================================
Coding & Code Quality
====================================================================
- Write small, focused functions (ideally no more than 50 executable lines or 4 parameters).
- Use async/await for asynchronous operations and handle errors with Raycast Toast notifications.
- Retain comments and documentation when refactoring code.
- Keep commit diffs as small as possible to facilitate safe integration.
- Follow naming conventions and coding standards set by the project’s ESLint and Prettier configurations.

====================================================================
Performance & Maintainability
====================================================================
- Optimize API usage by closely monitoring token consumption.
- Maintain a clear division between UI logic and core processing tasks.
- Validate and sanitize all inputs (e.g. clipboard text, image URLs) to prevent errors.
- Regularly refactor and clean the code (following the Boy-Scout Rule) to reduce technical debt.

====================================================================
Raycast API Integration & Configuration
====================================================================
- Always use Raycast API methods such as getPreferenceValues, showToast, closeMainWindow, and openExtensionPreferences.
- Register and configure all commands in package.json with clear titles, subtitles, and modes.
- Update user documentation whenever integration details change.
- For complete guidance on Raycast extension development, refer to the official Raycast Extension documentation:
  https://developers.raycast.com/

====================================================================
OpenAI API Integration & Configuration
====================================================================
- Use the official OpenAI npm package (e.g. version 4.20.1) for all API interactions.
- Securely retrieve your API key from Raycast preferences via getPreferenceValues and validate its existence.
- For text generation, use models such as "gpt-4o-mini" or "gpt-4o" (adjust based on user preferences) and update these identifiers as new versions become available.
- In image processing functions, replace outdated model identifiers (updating "gpt-4-vision-preview" to "gpt-4-vision") following current official guidelines.
- For text-to-speech conversion, continue using the designated model ("tts-1") and verify periodically for updated endpoints.
- Handle all API responses and errors gracefully using Raycast Toast notifications.
- Refer to the latest OpenAI documentation for model updates and usage guidelines:
  https://platform.openai.com/docs

====================================================================
Repository Structure
====================================================================
- The repository is organized as follows:
  • Root Files: Contains key files like README.md, CHANGELOG.md, package.json, tsconfig.json, .eslintrc.json, and .prettierrc.
  • assets/ and metadata/: Store images, icons, and other static resources along with project metadata.
  • src/: Contains the core application code:
      - const.ts, utills.ts: Define constants and utility functions.
      - read-clipboard-image.ts, read-selected-text-audio-only.ts, read-selected-text-script.tsx: Implement command functionality.
      - processors/: Houses specialized processor files such as image-to-text-processor.ts and text-to-speech-processor.ts.
  • .cursor/rules/: Contains the project-specific Cursor AI rules (e.g., project-rules.mdc).
- Adhere strictly to this structure when adding new files or refactoring existing components to ensure consistency and maintainability.
[1]

====================================================================
Debugging & Testing
====================================================================
- Include in-code comments for complex logic and maintain JSDoc comments for clarity.
- Use clear Toast notifications to signal both progress and completion of tasks.
- Write comprehensive unit tests for new processors and utilities to ensure stability.
- Verify that changes to background tasks or API calls are fully tested via Raycast extension commands.

====================================================================
Additional Rules
====================================================================
- Do not add placeholder or "TODO" comments in production code.
- If any requirement is unclear, seek clarification instead of making assumptions.
- All new code must adhere to the principles of modularity, clarity, and maintainability.
- Keep commit diffs as minimal as possible and document changes with clear commit messages.
- Regularly review and update both coding guidelines and integration settings as the repository evolves.